.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/punycode Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Punycode\&.js [![punycode on npm](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/punycode)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/punycode) [![](https://data.jsdelivr.com/v1/package/npm/punycode/badge)](https://www.jsdelivr.com/package/npm/punycode)"
.PP
Punycode\&.js is a robust Punycode converter that fully complies to \fRRFC 3492\fP and \fRRFC 5891\fP\&.

.PP
This JavaScript library is the result of comparing, optimizing and documenting different open-source implementations of the Punycode algorithm:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRThe C example code from RFC 3492\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRpunycode\&.c\fP by \fIMarkus W\&. Scherer\fP (IBM)\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRpunycode\&.c\fP by \fIBen Noordhuis\fP\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRJavaScript implementation by \fIsome\fP\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fRpunycode\&.js\fP by \fIBen Noordhuis\fP\fP (note: \fRnot fully compliant\fP)
.PP

.PP
This project was \fRbundled\fP with Node\&.js from \fRv0\&.6\&.2+\fP until \fRv7\fP (soft-deprecated)\&.

.PP
This project provides a CommonJS module that uses ES2015+ features and JavaScript module, which work in modern Node\&.js versions and browsers\&. For the old Punycode\&.js version that offers the same functionality in a UMD build with support for older pre-ES2015 runtimes, including Rhino, Ringo, and Narwhal, see \fRv1\&.4\&.1\fP\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
Via \fRnpm\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install punycode \-\-save
.fi
.PP

.PP
In \fRNode\&.js\fP:

.PP
.RS 4
⚠️ Note that userland modules don't hide core modules\&. For example, \fRrequire('punycode')\fP still imports the deprecated core module even if you executed \fRnpm install punycode\fP\&. Use \fRrequire('punycode/')\fP to import userland modules rather than core modules\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const punycode = require('punycode/');
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "\fRpunycode\&.decode(string)\fP"
Converts a Punycode string of ASCII symbols to a string of Unicode symbols\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// decode domain name parts
punycode\&.decode('maana\-pta'); // 'mañana'
punycode\&.decode('\-\-dqo34k'); // '☃\-⌘'
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRpunycode\&.encode(string)\fP"
Converts a string of Unicode symbols to a Punycode string of ASCII symbols\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// encode domain name parts
punycode\&.encode('mañana'); // 'maana\-pta'
punycode\&.encode('☃\-⌘'); // '\-\-dqo34k'
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRpunycode\&.toUnicode(input)\fP"
Converts a Punycode string representing a domain name or an email address to Unicode\&. Only the Punycoded parts of the input will be converted, i\&.e\&. it doesn’t matter if you call it on a string that has already been converted to Unicode\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// decode domain names
punycode\&.toUnicode('xn\-\-maana\-pta\&.com');
// → 'mañana\&.com'
punycode\&.toUnicode('xn\-\-\-\-dqo34k\&.com');
// → '☃\-⌘\&.com'

// decode email addresses
punycode\&.toUnicode('джумла@xn\-\-p\-8sbkgc5ag7bhce\&.xn\-\-ba\-lmcq');
// → 'джумла@джpумлатест\&.bрфa'
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRpunycode\&.toASCII(input)\fP"
Converts a lowercased Unicode string representing a domain name or an email address to Punycode\&. Only the non-ASCII parts of the input will be converted, i\&.e\&. it doesn’t matter if you call it with a domain that’s already in ASCII\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// encode domain names
punycode\&.toASCII('mañana\&.com');
// → 'xn\-\-maana\-pta\&.com'
punycode\&.toASCII('☃\-⌘\&.com');
// → 'xn\-\-\-\-dqo34k\&.com'

// encode email addresses
punycode\&.toASCII('джумла@джpумлатест\&.bрфa');
// → 'джумла@xn\-\-p\-8sbkgc5ag7bhce\&.xn\-\-ba\-lmcq'
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRpunycode\&.ucs2\fP"
.SS "\fRpunycode\&.ucs2\&.decode(string)\fP"
Creates an array containing the numeric code point values of each Unicode symbol in the string\&. While \fRJavaScript uses UCS-2 internally\fP, this function will convert a pair of surrogate halves (each of which UCS-2 exposes as separate characters) into a single code point, matching UTF-16\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
punycode\&.ucs2\&.decode('abc');
// → [0x61, 0x62, 0x63]
// surrogate pair for U+1D306 TETRAGRAM FOR CENTRE:
punycode\&.ucs2\&.decode('\\uD834\\uDF06');
// → [0x1D306]
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRpunycode\&.ucs2\&.encode(codePoints)\fP"
Creates a string based on an array of numeric code point values\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
punycode\&.ucs2\&.encode([0x61, 0x62, 0x63]);
// → 'abc'
punycode\&.ucs2\&.encode([0x1D306]);
// → '\\uD834\\uDF06'
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRpunycode\&.version\fP"
A string representing the current Punycode\&.js version number\&.
.SH "For maintainers"
.PP
.SS "How to publish a new release"
.IP "1." 4
On the \fRmain\fP branch, bump the version number in \fRpackage\&.json\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm version patch \-m 'Release v%s'
.fi
.PP

.PP
Instead of \fRpatch\fP, use \fRminor\fP or \fRmajor\fP \fRas needed\fP\&.

.PP
Note that this produces a Git commit + tag\&.
.PP
.IP "1." 4
Push the release commit and tag:

.PP
.PP
.nf
git push && git push \-\-tags
.fi
.PP

.PP
Our CI then automatically publishes the new release to npm, under both the \fR\fRpunycode\fP\fP and \fR\fRpunycode\&.js\fP\fP names\&.
.PP
